/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"launchkey-level-6","number":6,"title":"Velocity Sensitive RGB Pad Subsystems","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"The 16 RGB velocity-sensitive pads on the Launchkey MK4 61 function as a dedicated, low-latency percussion and sample triggering surface in Drum Mode. This module covers the hardware layout, standardized chromatic pitch mapping, channel rou","modules":[{"id":"launchkey-m-6-1-1","code":"6.1","title":"Velocity-Sensitive RGB Pad Subsystems","blurb":"The 16 RGB velocity-sensitive pads on the Launchkey MK4 61 function as a dedicated, low-latency percussion and sample triggering surface in Drum Mode. This module covers the hardware layout, standardized chromatic pitch mapping, channel rou","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o-16","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 6.1 — Drum Performance Pad Mode","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"The 16 RGB velocity-sensitive pads on the Launchkey MK4 61 function as a dedicated, low-latency percussion and sample triggering surface in <strong>Drum Mode</strong>. This module covers the hardware layout, standardized chromatic pitch mapping, channel routing, dynamic visual feedback, and the architectural concept of the 2×8 pad window."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Engage Drum Mode using the standardized Shift-pad selection protocol.","Formulate the precise 16-pad chromatic note map (C1 through D♯2) and verify transmission on dedicated MIDI Channel 10.","Contrast standalone pad illumination (Static Blue) with host-integrated DAW color tracking.","Formulate the architectural concept of the 2×8 pad matrix as a movable \"window\" into larger software sampler matrices (e.g., Ableton Live Drum Racks)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-6-1-1","title":"6.1.1: Drum Mode Selection & Default Status","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.1: Drum Mode Selection & Default Status","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Drum Mode is the factory default power-on pad mode of the Launchkey MK4 61."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Manual Selection</strong>: Press and hold <code>Shift</code>, then press <strong>Pad 10</strong> (silkscreened <code>Drum</code>).","<strong>OLED Display Confirmation</strong>: The screen temporarily displays <code>Pad Mode: Drum</code>.","<strong>Shift Navigation Illumination</strong>: When <code>Shift</code> is held, Pad 10 illuminates in <strong>solid bright blue</strong>, indicating it is the currently active pad engine."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-6-1-2","title":"6.1.2: Authoritative 16-Pad Pitch Mapping","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.2: Authoritative 16-Pad Pitch Mapping","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Drum Mode, the pads transmit standard MIDI <code>Note On</code> and <code>Note Off</code> messages on <strong>MIDI Channel 10</strong> (the universal General MIDI percussion channel)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                    DRUM MODE 16-PAD PITCH MAPPING                             |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| TOP ROW (Pads 1–8):                                                           |\n| [Pad 1: E1]  [Pad 2: F1]  [Pad 3: F#1] [Pad 4: G1]  [Pad 5: G#1] [Pad 6: A1]  [Pad 7: A#1] [Pad 8: B1] |\n| (Note 40)    (Note 41)    (Note 42)    (Note 43)    (Note 44)    (Note 45)    (Note 46)    (Note 47)  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| BOTTOM ROW (Pads 9–16):                                                       |\n| [Pad 9: C1]  [Pad 10: C#1][Pad 11: D1] [Pad 12: D#1][Pad 13: E1] [Pad 14: F1] [Pad 15: F#1][Pad 16: G1]*|\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Chromatic Layout Detail (Bottom-Left to Top-Right progression)</em>:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Bottom Row (Pads 9–16)</strong>: Covers <strong>C1 (MIDI Note 36)</strong> through <strong>B1 (MIDI Note 47)</strong> in chromatic steps.","<strong>Top Row (Pads 1–8)</strong>: Covers <strong>C2 (MIDI Note 48)</strong> through <strong>D♯2 (MIDI Note 51)</strong> across the pad array.","<em>Standard Drum Rack Alignment</em>: Pad 9 (Bottom-Left) corresponds to the standard primary Bass Drum / Kick (<code>C1</code>, Note 36); Pad 11 corresponds to the standard Acoustic Snare (<code>D1</code>, Note 38); Pad 12 / Pad 3 correspond to Closed/Open Hi-Hats."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-6-1-3","title":"6.1.3: Visual Telemetry & Color Integration","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.3: Visual Telemetry & Color Integration","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Standalone / Hardware Mode</strong>: When no DAW is connected, all 16 pads illuminate in a uniform <strong>deep blue</strong>. Striking a pad causes it to flash <strong>bright white/blue</strong> during the strike duration.","<strong>DAW Integrated Mode</strong>: When connected to a supported DAW (e.g., Ableton Live):","The pads dynamically inherit the <strong>Track Color</strong> of the active Drum Rack track.","Populated drum cells illuminate in the track color; empty/unmapped cells illuminate dimly or remain unlit.","Striking a pad triggers instantaneous velocity-scaled flash feedback."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-6-1-4","title":"6.1.4: The 2×8 Pad Window Paradigm","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.4: The 2×8 Pad Window Paradigm","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"An Ableton Live Drum Rack contains a total matrix of <strong>128 potential pad cells</strong> (spanning notes C-2 to G8). The Launchkey MK4 61 provides 16 physical pads arranged as a 2×8 grid."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">       ABLETON LIVE DRUM RACK (128 PADS)\n+-----------------------------------------------+\n| C5 – G8    [ Unfocused Pad Matrix Area ]      |\n| C3 – B4    [ Unfocused Pad Matrix Area ]      |\n| C1 – D#2  ┌─────────────────────────────────┐ | ◄── [ Launchkey 2x8 Window ]\n|           │ [Pad 1: E1] ... [Pad 8: D#2]    │ |\n|           │ [Pad 9: C1] ... [Pad 16: B1]    │ |\n|           └─────────────────────────────────┘ |\n| C-2 – B0   [ Unfocused Pad Matrix Area ]      |\n+-----------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Window Navigation</strong>: The physical 16-pad matrix acts as a focused 16-pad viewport into the DAW Drum Rack.","<strong>Scrolling the Matrix</strong>: Moving focus across the 128-pad Drum Rack is performed using the <code>Pad Bank Up / Down</code> buttons or selecting drum pads on the computer screen."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: Drum mode selection (<code>Shift + Pad 10</code>); exact note span C1–D♯2 on dedicated Channel 10; color states (standalone blue vs DAW track color); 2×8 window paradigm into 128-pad racks.","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Speculation regarding 8×8 Push-style layouts; claiming the 16 pads physically display all 128 drum notes simultaneously."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Practical Guided Task & Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Task</strong>: Engage Drum Mode (<code>Shift + Pad 10</code>). In your DAW MIDI monitor or track input monitor, strike Pad 9 (bottom-left) and Pad 8 (top-right). Confirm the exact MIDI note numbers and channel received."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Checkpoint Validation</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["Pad 9 transmits MIDI Note 36 (C1) on Channel 10.","Pad 8 transmits MIDI Note 51 (D♯2) on Channel 10.","Pads flash with dynamic visual intensity corresponding to strike velocity."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"launchkey-m-6-2-2","code":"6.2","title":"Velocity-Sensitive RGB Pad Subsystems","blurb":"Each of the 16 RGB silicone pads on the Launchkey MK4 61 is equipped with continuous mechanical pressure transducers supporting Polyphonic Aftertouch. This module details the distinction between Polyphonic and Channel Aftertouch, pressure t","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o-17","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 6.2 — Pad Pressure & Polyphonic Aftertouch Engine","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Each of the 16 RGB silicone pads on the Launchkey MK4 61 is equipped with continuous mechanical pressure transducers supporting <strong>Polyphonic Aftertouch</strong>. This module details the distinction between Polyphonic and Channel Aftertouch, pressure threshold configuration, and the critical architectural warning regarding MIDI Polyphonic Expression (MPE) channel allocation in host DAWs."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Formulate the MIDI byte architecture of Polyphonic Key Pressure versus Channel Pressure.","Configure continuous aftertouch sensing modes and pressure activation thresholds in Settings.","Formulate the technical difference between Polyphonic Aftertouch and full 3D MPE surfaces.","Explain Novation’s official warning regarding MPE channel exhaustion in Ableton Live."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-6-2-1","title":"6.2.1: Polyphonic Aftertouch vs. Channel Pressure","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.2.1: Polyphonic Aftertouch vs. Channel Pressure","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"After initial velocity strike, applying continuous downward pressure on a pad triggers MIDI Aftertouch data:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  AFTERTOUCH PROTOCOL ARCHITECTURE                       |\n+-------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------+\n| Aftertouch Type   | MIDI Status Byte    | Operational Data Structure    |\n+-------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **Polyphonic**    | `0xA0` – `0xAF`     | Byte 1: Specific Note Number  |\n| **Aftertouch**    | (Ch 1 – 16)         | Byte 2: Pressure (0 – 127)    |\n| *(Factory Default)*|                    | *Modulates each pad discretely|\n+-------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **Channel**       | `0xD0` – `0xDF`     | Byte 1: Pressure (0 – 127)    |\n| **Aftertouch**    | (Ch 1 – 16)         | *Applies highest pad pressure |\n|                   |                     |  uniformly to all held notes  |\n+-------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **Off**           | None                | Pressure sensors deactivated  |\n+-------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Musical Benefit of Polyphonic Aftertouch</em>: When holding a 4-note chord or multiple drum pads, pressing down on a single pad modulates filter cutoff or vibrato for <strong>only that specific voice/pad</strong>, leaving other sustaining voices completely unmodulated."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-6-2-2","title":"6.2.2: Pressure Engagement Thresholds","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.2.2: Pressure Engagement Thresholds","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Settings -&gt; <code>Pad AT Threshold</code>:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Low</strong>: Engages aftertouch data streaming with very light resting pressure.","<strong>Normal (Default)</strong>: Balanced mechanical response; requires deliberate downward pressure.","<strong>High</strong>: Requires forceful pressure to exceed the activation gate (recommended for fast, aggressive finger-drumming where accidental modulation must be avoided)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-6-2-3","title":"6.2.3: Polyphonic Aftertouch vs. Full MPE Controllers","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.2.3: Polyphonic Aftertouch vs. Full MPE Controllers","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A frequent point of industry confusion is equating Polyphonic Aftertouch with a full <strong>MIDI Polyphonic Expression (MPE)</strong> surface (e.g., LinnStrument, Roli Seaboard, Expressive E Osmose)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                     EXPRESSION SURFACE COMPARISON                       |\n+---------------------+-----------------------+---------------------------+\n| Expressive Axis     | Launchkey MK4 61 Pads | Full MPE Controller       |\n+---------------------+-----------------------+---------------------------+\n| **X-Axis (Strike)** | Velocity (1–127)      | Velocity (1–127)          |\n| **Z-Axis (Press)**  | **Polyphonic AT (Z)** | **Polyphonic Pressure (Z)**|\n| **X-Axis (Glide)**  | None (Static pads)    | **Continuous Pitch Glide**|\n| **Y-Axis (Slide)**  | None                  | **Vertical Finger Slide** |\n+---------------------+-----------------------+---------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Classification Rule</em>: The Launchkey MK4 61 is <strong>not</strong> an MPE controller; it is a velocity-sensitive controller featuring <strong>Polyphonic Aftertouch</strong> on its 16 pads."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-6-2-4","title":"6.2.4: Novation's Official Ableton Live MPE Warning","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.2.4: Novation's Official Ableton Live MPE Warning","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Ableton Live's MIDI Preferences (<code>Link, Tempo &amp; MIDI</code>), each MIDI input port features an optional <code>MPE</code> checkbox."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                CRITICAL LIVE 12 MPE WARNING (NOVATION)                  |\n|                                                                         |\n|  Enabling the \"MPE\" checkbox on the Launchkey MK4 MIDI Input port in    |\n|  Ableton Live forces the DAW to treat incoming data as a multi-channel  |\n|  MPE stream across Channels 2–16.                                       |\n|                                                                         |\n|  CONSEQUENCE:                                                           |\n|  Individual Live tracks can no longer listen to discrete MIDI channels. |\n|  Multi-timbral segregation (Keybed Part A on Ch 1, Part B on Ch 2,      |\n|  Chords on Ch 3, Drums on Ch 10) COMPLETELY BREAKS DOWN.                |\n|                                                                         |\n|  MANDATORY DIRECTIVE:                                                   |\n|  Leave the \"MPE\" checkbox OFF for all Launchkey MK4 ports in Ableton    |\n|  Live MIDI preferences unless operating an isolated single-track MPE    |\n|  synth patch where multi-channel track isolation is not required.       |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: Polyphonic Aftertouch byte structure (<code>0xA0</code>) vs Channel (<code>0xD0</code>) vs Off; AT Thresholds (Low/Normal/High); clear distinction that MK4 pads are Poly AT and NOT full MPE; Novation MPE channel-exhaustion warning in Ableton Live.","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Calling the Launchkey MK4 an MPE controller; recommending enabling MPE without documenting channel conflicts."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Practical Guided Task & Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Task</strong>: In Settings, verify <code>Pad Aftertouch</code> is set to <code>Polyphonic</code>. Route the Launchkey into a software synth capable of receiving Polyphonic Key Pressure (e.g., Ableton Wavetable or Drift with Poly Aftertouch mapped to Filter Cutoff). Hold two pads, apply pressure to one, and observe discrete per-voice modulation."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Checkpoint Validation</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["MIDI monitor confirms discrete <code>0xA0</code> poly-pressure packets with independent note byte addressing.","Ableton Live MIDI Preferences confirm the <code>MPE</code> checkbox is <strong>OFF</strong>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"launchkey-m-6-3-3","code":"6.3","title":"Velocity-Sensitive RGB Pad Subsystems","blurb":"User Chord is a dedicated hardware pad mode that enables performers to store custom polyphonic harmonic voicings (up to 6 notes per pad) across all 16 RGB pads. Once stored, complex multi-finger voicings can be triggered instantaneously wit","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o-18","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 6.3 — User Chord Custom Pad Mapping","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"User Chord is a dedicated hardware pad mode that enables performers to store custom polyphonic harmonic voicings (up to 6 notes per pad) across all 16 RGB pads. Once stored, complex multi-finger voicings can be triggered instantaneously with a single pad press. This module details voicing capture, OLED chord detection, bank transposition, chord erasure, and channel routing."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Engage User Chord pad mode via the Shift-modifier protocol.","Store custom multi-note voicings (up to the 6-note hardware ceiling) onto unassigned pads.","Transpose stored chord banks in semitone steps and octave increments using pad bank controls.","Execute chord erasure using the <code>Function</code> button protocol.","Formulate the default MIDI Channel 3 routing for all chord engines.","Complete Assessment Gate E."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-6-3-1","title":"6.3.1: User Chord Mode Initialization","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.1: User Chord Mode Initialization","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"User Chord operates as a primary pad performance engine:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Activation</strong>: Hold <code>Shift</code> and press <strong>Pad 11</strong> (silkscreened <code>User Chord</code>).","<strong>Initial Pad States</strong>:","<em>Unassigned (Empty) Pads</em>: Extinguished / completely unlit.","<em>Assigned (Populated) Pads</em>: Illuminated in bright blue."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-6-3-2","title":"6.3.2: Storing Custom Voicings & The 6-Note Limit","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.2: Storing Custom Voicings & The 6-Note Limit","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Launchkey MK4 61 captures chord voicings directly from physical keybed input:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">CHORD CAPTURE STEP-BY-STEP WORKFLOW:\n1. Press and HOLD an unlit (empty) pad in the 2x8 grid.\n2. While holding the pad, play between 1 and 6 notes on the keybed.\n   - Notes can be played simultaneously as a block chord or pressed sequentially.\n3. Release the pad.\n4. The chord is now permanently stored in internal RAM/EEPROM.</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">       OLED CHORD RECOGNITION DISPLAY\n+----------------------------------------+\n|  USER CHORD: Pad 9                     |\n|  [ C Maj9 ]                            |\n|  Notes: C3, E3, G3, B3, D4             |\n+----------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Hardware Polyphony Ceiling</em>: The internal User Chord engine stores a maximum of <strong>6 notes per pad</strong>. If 7 or more keys are depressed during capture, the microcontroller stores the first 6 notes and discards any subsequent notes."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-6-3-3","title":"6.3.3: Bank Transposition in Semitones & Octaves","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.3: Bank Transposition in Semitones & Octaves","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Once voicings are mapped across the pads, the entire 16-pad collection can be transposed globally without re-programming individual pads:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  USER CHORD BANK TRANSPOSITION MATRIX                   |\n+-------------------+--------------------+--------------------------------+\n| Hardware Command  | Transposition Step | Maximum Permitted Range        |\n+-------------------+--------------------+--------------------------------+\n| [ Pad Bank ▲ / ▼ ]| **±1 Semitone**    | Up to **±12 Semitones** (1 Oct)|\n+-------------------+--------------------+--------------------------------+\n| [ Shift ] +       | **±1 Octave**      | Up to **±3 Octaves**           |\n| [ Pad Bank ▲ / ▼ ]| (12 Semitones)     |                                |\n+-------------------+--------------------+--------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-6-3-4","title":"6.3.4: Dedicated Chord Deletion Protocol","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.4: Dedicated Chord Deletion Protocol","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To erase a chord voicing and return a pad to its blank state:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">CHORD ERASURE WORKFLOW:\n1. Press and HOLD the [ Function ] button.\n2. The OLED screen displays: \"Delete Chord!\".\n3. All populated chord pads immediately turn BRIGHT RED.\n4. Press the RED pad you wish to erase.\n5. The pad LED extinguishes (returns to blank state).\n6. Release [ Function ].</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-6-3-5","title":"6.3.5: Default Channel 3 Routing","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.3.5: Default Channel 3 Routing","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"All chord engines on the Launchkey MK4 61 (User Chord, Chord Map, and Fixed Chord) transmit MIDI data on <strong>MIDI Channel 3</strong> (configurable via Settings -&gt; <code>Chords Ch</code>)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Routing Directive</em>: If a DAW track or hardware synthesizer is set to listen strictly to Channel 1 (the keybed channel), triggering User Chord pads will produce no sound. The target track must be set to receive <strong>MIDI Channel 3</strong> (or <strong>All Channels</strong>)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: Mode entry (<code>Shift + Pad 11</code>); chord capture workflow; 6-note hardware ceiling; OLED chord recognition; Semitone (<code>Pad Bank Up/Down</code>) and Octave (<code>Shift + Bank</code>) transposition; Function-held red pad deletion; default Channel 3 routing.","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Confusing User Chord with Generative Chord Map (covered in Module 9)."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Assessment Gate E (Pad Engines & Aftertouch Validation)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Objective</strong>: Demonstrate complete operational mastery of Drum Mode, Polyphonic Aftertouch, and User Chord capture."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Self-Assessment Checklist</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Select Drum Mode (<code>Shift + Pad 10</code>) and verify Pad 9 outputs Note 36 (C1) on Channel 10.","[ ] Verify Polyphonic Aftertouch generates independent per-pad pressure data in a MIDI monitor.","[ ] State the Ableton Live MPE channel-occupancy warning in a single clear technical sentence.","[ ] Select User Chord Mode (<code>Shift + Pad 11</code>), program a 4-note chord on Pad 9, verify the OLED chord name, transpose the bank +2 semitones, and then execute a <code>Function</code>-held deletion to restore the pad to blank.","[ ] Confirm User Chord transmits on MIDI Channel 3."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
